# What the getting-started walk vouches for: a change touching any of these
# runs it in the harness's per-run check stage, and a change touching none of
# them does not, once .yoyodyne/config.yaml names the walk under `path_checks`.
# This file keeps its old name, and `make adoption` its old target, only because
# .yoyodyne/config.yaml names both there; the Makefile's `adoption` target says
# what replaces them. A change that edits this file runs the walk whatever the file
# then says, so no change can narrow the check that holds it.
#
# One pattern a line, in the part of .gitignore's syntax everybody reads: no "/"
# matches any component, a "/" anchors at the repository root, a trailing "/"
# means a directory and everything under it, and "!" takes back what an earlier
# line covered, the last matching line deciding. docs/developing-yoyo.md says
# when the walk runs and why.

# What the walk reads and executes.
/README.md
/scripts/walk-getting-started.sh
/scripts/walk-adoption.paths
/Makefile
/go.mod
/go.sum
# The tracker version the walk fetches where a machine has none.
/.github/workflows/ci.yml

# The program the README documents: the walk builds it and runs it, so a change
# in what it does can make a documented claim false without touching the README.
# That is how pull requests 902 and 907 turned main red.
/cmd/
/internal/

# Tests and their fixtures are not in the binary the walk builds.
!*_test.go
!testdata/
